# Slugs that have been renamed. One hop, never a chain: every entry points
# at the guide's current URL, not at the slug that replaced it.

# Trailing-slash rules removed: /x -> /x/ is a redirect loop on Netlify.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Slugs this build did not generate — must stay last.
#
# Not forced, so Netlify serves a real generated file whenever one exists
# and only falls through to here when it does not. Two things reach this
# line, and they need opposite treatment:
#
#   1. A guide added in Studio since the last deploy. It exists in
#      Firestore but has no page yet. It must render.
#   2. A slug that genuinely does not exist. It must not be indexed.
#
# Netlify cannot tell them apart — it does not know what is in Firestore.
# So this rewrites to guide.html, which does know: it reads the slug from
# the path and looks it up. Found, it renders the guide. Not found, it
# writes a noindex tag and shows the not-found message, so a dead URL
# still cannot end up in the index.
#
# This is the one place the architecture accepts a soft 404 rather than a
# hard one. The alternative was new guides 404ing until the next deploy,
# which is a far worse failure and one that happens far more often.
/guides/*  /guide.html  200
